+// A looser proximity gate used only to decide whether kick() should fire.
+//
+// isTouchingBall() requires the ball to sit within a razor-thin box flush
+// against the kicker face (xx < kickerThickness*2 + ballRadius ~= 3.15cm
+// forward, yy < kickerWidth*0.5 = 4cm lateral, with kickerThickness = 5mm)
+// -- appropriate for deciding whether to grab the ball into the dribbler
+// hold (holdBall()), but far stricter than what any Python-side "do I have
+// the ball" sensor actually checks before issuing a kick command (e.g. the
+// visual has_ball() capture_distance used by utama_core's tactics is a
+// 0.15m *radial*, center-to-center distance -- not an axis-aligned box at
+// all). While the ball is held (holdingBall via holdBall()'s free-swinging
+// hinge to the kicker box), it can legitimately drift outside
+// isTouchingBall()'s thin window -- e.g. during a fast turn_on_spot pivot,
+// or while a robot is still settling after receiving a pass -- while
+// remaining visually/physically "with" the robot by any reasonable
+// definition. If kick() gates on isTouchingBall() and that check happens
+// to be false on the exact tick the kick command arrives, kick() silently
+// no-ops (see below): unholdBall() still releases the ball, but no
+// directional velocity is ever applied, so the ball simply keeps whatever
+// residual momentum it already had -- in a direction unrelated to the
+// robot's facing angle. This was confirmed to reproduce large (tens of
+// degrees to wildly wrong) launch-direction mismatches between the
+// commanded orientation and the ball's actual post-kick flight path.
+//
+// isNearKickerFace() instead uses a plain radial distance from the chassis
+// center, matched to that same 0.15m real-world capture distance, plus a
+// sanity check that the ball is roughly in front of the robot (not
+// directly behind it, where a "kick" would be physically nonsensical) so a
+// kick that Python's own sensors would already consider "in possession"
+// reliably fires.
+bool SSLRobot::Kicker::isNearKickerFace()
+{
+    dReal vx,vy,vz;
+    dReal bx,by,bz;
+    dReal rx,ry,rz;
+    dReal kx,ky,kz;
+
+    this->rob->chassis->getBodyDirection(vx,vy,vz);
+    this->rob->getBall()->getBodyPosition(bx,by,bz);
+    // Planar (x/y) distance and forward-facing check are measured from the
+    // chassis center, matching Python's has_ball(visual=True) semantics
+    // exactly (robot.p.distance_to(ball.p) against the robot's own
+    // position) -- not from the kicker box's position, which sits ~8cm
+    // forward of the chassis center and made a ball that's clearly still
+    // within capture range of the robot overall (e.g. approaching fast,
+    // just shy of the kicker's own forward offset) incorrectly fail a
+    // dot-product-from-the-kicker forward check.
+    this->rob->chassis->getBodyPosition(rx,ry,rz);
+    // Height is still checked against the kicker box's own z -- it sits
+    // near ball height close to the ground, well below the chassis's
+    // vertical center, so comparing height against the chassis center
+    // would reject essentially every real ball position.
+    this->box->getBodyPosition(kx,ky,kz);
+
+    dReal ox = bx - rx;
+    dReal oy = by - ry;
+    dReal dist = sqrt(ox*ox + oy*oy);
+    dReal zz = fabs(kz - bz);
+
+    const dReal kKickCaptureDistance = 0.15; // matches has_ball()'s visual capture_distance
+    // Forward-facing component of the offset -- reject a ball that's behind
+    // the robot rather than in front of it.
+    dReal forward = ox*vx + oy*vy;
+
+    return (dist < kKickCaptureDistance) && (forward > 0) &&
+           (zz < SSLConfig::Robot().getKickerHeight() * 0.5f);
+}
